{"evidence":[{"sentence_id":0,"text":"The 2010 Tampa Bay Buccaneers season was the club's 35th season as a franchise in the NFL.","title":"2010 Tampa Bay Buccaneers season"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" The Buccaneers entered the season attempting to improve on their 3–13 record and last place finish in the NFC South in 2009, a feat they accomplished after only six games.","title":"2010 Tampa Bay Buccaneers season"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" The Buccaneers achieved the best turnaround in franchise history and became the first team since the NFL merger in 1970 to start 10 rookies and achieve a winning season.","title":"2010 Tampa Bay Buccaneers season"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" Raheem Morris spent his second season as head coach.","title":"2010 Tampa Bay Buccaneers season"},{"sentence_id":4,"text":" The Buccaneers had the third overall pick in the 2010 NFL Draft, with which they selected Gerald McCoy.","title":"2010 Tampa Bay Buccaneers season"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Gerald Keith McCoy Jr. (born February 25, 1988) is an American football defensive tackle for the Tampa Bay Buccaneers of the National Football League (NFL).","title":"Gerald McCoy"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" He played college football at Oklahoma, where he earned consensus All-American honors, and was drafted by the Buccaneers third overall in the 2010 NFL Draft.","title":"Gerald McCoy"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" Since joining the Buccaneers and the NFL, he has been selected to the Pro Bowl five consecutive years.","title":"Gerald McCoy"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"David Logan (October 25, 1956 – January 13, 1999) was an American football defensive tackle in the National Football League.","title":"David Logan (American football)"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" He was drafted by the Tampa Bay Buccaneers in the 12th round of the 1979 NFL Draft.","title":"David Logan (American football)"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" He played college football at Pittsburgh.","title":"David Logan (American football)"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" A nose tackle with the Tampa Bay Buccaneers from 1979 to 1986, Logan finished his career ranked second on the team's all-time sack list and sixth in career tackles.","title":"David Logan (American football)"},{"sentence_id":4,"text":" Named an All-Pro in 1984 and alternate three times in his career, finishing his NFL playing career with the Green Bay Packers in 1987.","title":"David Logan (American football)"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Brian Larisso Price (born April 10, 1989) is a former American football defensive tackle in the National Football League for the Tampa Bay Buccaneers.","title":"Brian Price (American football, born 1989)"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" After playing college football at the University of California, Los Angeles, he was drafted by the Tampa Bay Buccaneers in the second round of the 2010 NFL Draft.","title":"Brian Price (American football, born 1989)"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Santana N. Dotson (born December 19, 1969 in New Orleans, Louisiana) is a former American football defensive tackle in the National Football League.","title":"Santana Dotson"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" He was a part of Houston's Yates High School football team when it won the 1985 5A state championship.","title":"Santana Dotson"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" While at Baylor, Dotson was voted All-American in 1991.","title":"Santana Dotson"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" He won the 1992 NFL Defensive Rookie of the Year with the Tampa Bay Buccaneers and played in two Super Bowls with the Green Bay Packers.","title":"Santana Dotson"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Stevie Tu'ikolovatu (born June 28, 1991) is an American football defensive tackle for the Tampa Bay Buccaneers of the National Football League (NFL).","title":"Stevie Tu'ikolovatu"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" He played college football at Utah before transferring to USC.","title":"Stevie Tu'ikolovatu"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" He is the nephew of Sione Po'uha who was a defensive tackle drafted by the New York Jets in 2005.","title":"Stevie Tu'ikolovatu"}],"peer_messages":["Unverified peer answer: Gerald McCoy
